<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirunavaya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thirunavaya is a village in Malappuram district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, on the north bank of the Bharathappuzha river. It is known for its Navamukunda temple and as the venue of the Mamankam, the great twelve-yearly festival presided over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorins</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> until 1755, at which rival warriors from Valluvanad challenged the Zamorin's authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirunavaya.jpg" alt="Thirunavaya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssriram mt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thirunavaya_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
